The `no-param-reassign` eslint rule helps enforce immutability in code by preventing the reassignment of function parameters. This can help prevent unintended consequences and hard-to-debug issues. 

Example: 

```javascript
function exampleFunction(obj) {
  obj.property = 'new value'; // violates no-param-reassign rule
}

const obj = { property: 'old value' };
exampleFunction(obj);
console.log(obj.property); // logs 'new value'
```

Without the `no-param-reassign` rule, the `exampleFunction` above would be allowed to change the value of the `obj` parameter, which can lead to unexpected behavior. With the rule enabled, the function would need to create a new object instead:

```javascript
function exampleFunction(obj) {
  const newObj = { ...obj, property: 'new value' };
  return newObj;
}

const obj = { property: 'old value' };
const newObj = exampleFunction(obj);
console.log(obj.property); // logs 'old value'
console.log(newObj.property); // logs 'new value'
```

